h your data there's  going to be an issue with your model  check the distributions of your data  check the features the ranges the  distributions etc against what you know  other subject matter matter experts you  know within you you know your business  your industry or company are saying  about the data if there's disparities  between what you found and what people  are saying you should find be able to  account for that maybe there's an issue  with your ETL or you need to revisit  like the data understanding the business  understanding but again this is one of  those things that in terms of workflow  implement it early to keep you from  wasting lots of cycles trying to get a  model to fit the data that's not you  know that has some sort of fundamental  issue so just for example recently I was  working on a project and I came across  something like this where it was like a  binary classification project so I  basically plotted the distribution  separated by you know the label 0 and  the label 1 of a certain feature and  this feature I thought would be very  useful for actually predicting the  outcome so I expected something like  this I expected the distributions of  this feature to be very different  depending on what class it belonged to  but in reality it was something like  this they were nearly identical which  explains why you know I had trouble  fitting the model so this is something  good to implement early you know again  test your assumptions what I learned  from this was ok I didn't quite  understand the date of the way that I  thought I did I need to go  and you know pull it again differently  another thing that I think is very  useful is to establish a baseline so I  would say like a good baseline is easy  to implement easy to understand and it's  simple but it's not a straw man right so  this should be when I think of a  baseline it's like something that if you  had to you could probably deploy within  a sprint and you would be confident that  it's working correctly and this is just  you know your base performance if any  more complicated model can't you know  beat your baseline then maybe you need  to go back and revisit the data  understanding revisit the business  understanding or or you know maybe you  just don't have the the resources you  need to be able to solve the problem at  that time where you need to frame the  problem differently so some examples a  simple heuristic so a concrete example  of this is I used to work in healthcare  and one of the models that we were  interested in there was if somebody had  this disease last year will he have it  this year and so you know a heuristic  for that is there's lots of diseases and  conditions that we know are chronic so  we could say well if this person had  this condition last year this chronic  condition we assume they have it this  year you know it's kind of on the  borderline of that like simple but kind  of a straw man because you know it's not  going to work for all the conditions but  it still kind of gets you in the  ballpark of what you should expect a  model to be able to be another example  is we predict ratings at Caden and so  one of the things is like that's just a  simple heuristic to form a baseline is  you know well let's just assume that  like the ratings on cable aren't really  changing much week over week so let's  use last week's we rating and use that  as our prediction and be honest it's a  it's a pretty good baseline to use  because they actually don't change much  week over week there's things like  seasonality where that that approach  will break down but again it gives you a  ballpark of where you want to be  an interpretable model is another great  example so you know something like a  linear model logistic regression linear  regression something like a tree based  model where you could inspect the  features again just to give you an idea  that you're on the right track so  another example I won't really go into  the details of th