ss value or  business terminology so for example you  know mean absolute percent error is nice  it converts nicely into business terms  you can say yeah our predicted bid  prices are within five percent of the  winning bid price and you know that's  easy to disseminate that information  among other teams so when possible try  and do that and comparability choose  metrics that can be compared across time  across data sets across iterations you  want to not all metrics can do that and  so you want to make sure that what  you're looking at is really comparing  apples to apples if you're going to roll  out a new version of your model you want  to make sure that the criteria it's  being evaluated on makes sense compared  to the previous iteration so think about  deployment and future iterations before  you start development I worked with  somebody who once said nothing is more  permanent than temporary  and I think that's a great quote so  prepare for deployment and future  iterations so for example if you want to  roll out your model the next iteration  using like a/b testing or something like  that think about what sort of  infrastructure requirements that has  what sort of requirements is that going  to impose on your actual API you'll  probably need to be like if you're  exposing your model behind the REST API  for example you'll probably need to be  passing back model ID or something like  that so you can track the performance  from one one version of the model verse  another  and think about the feedback loop this  is essential right I've worked on many  many projects in different industries  where this is not as straightforward as  you would like to think that it is it's  not always easy to tie the outcome to  the model predictions for a variety of  reasons and there's a lot of subtleties  here but a couple of examples are like  ad recommendations a user can't click on  an ad that they weren't shown so your  model might predict across the bunch of  ads but if they're only showing one they  can only click one you know if you don't  think about this carefully you may never  be able to evaluate how you know maybe  your second best predictions are doing  in production or another example suppose  you work for a hospital and you have a  model that's predicting if somebody's  likely going to be readmitted to the  hospital in the next 60 days and the  hospital goes and takes that prediction  and people who are classified as yes  they will be back within the next 60  days the hospital tries to do an  intervention you know some sort of  measure to keep them from coming back so  there in fact there you know the use  case of the model is essentially trying  to change the predicted label right so  now you might end up asking yourself and  you look at the results is the model  that bad or that good right so you may  have a lot of predictions and these  people never came back but maybe it was  that good you identified the people that  were going to come back and the  intervention prevented them from doing  so right so again these are things to  think about in the end the end process  so this brings us to during development  this is probably the most generalizable  phase and I think it's a good  opportunity like amongst the team to  adopt and standardize  best practices I think I said in the  abstract of this talk they've like these  things are better thought of as like a  playbook than a rule book but sometimes  it is good to have sort of like a  foundation of principles so there's a  couple other data scientists from cadent  here  with me and this is one of the things  that we've tried to do is you know  within reason to find some best  practices for how well valuate models so  the first thing is test your assumptions  write garbage in garbage out we all know  that this is probably the thing that I  like and least inclined to do I want to  get it data set and fit it right away  but this is super important you know if  there's an issue with your data there's  going to be an issue with your model  check the distributions of your data  check the features the ranges the  distributions etc against what you know  other subject matter matter experts you  know within you you know your business  your industry or company are saying  about the data if there's disparities  between what you found and what people  are saying you should find be able to  account for that maybe there's an issue  with your ETL or you need to revisit  like the data understanding the business  understanding but again this is one of  those things that in terms of workflow  implement it early to keep you from  wasting lots of cycles trying to get a  model to fit the data that's not you  know that has some sort of fundamental  issue so just for example recently I was  working on a project and I came across  something like this where it was like a  binary classification project so I  basically plotted the distribution  separated by you know the label 0 and  the label 1 of a certain feature and  this feature I thought would be very  useful for actually predicting the  outcome so I expected something like  this I expected the distributions of  this feature to be very different  depending on what class it belonged to  but in reality it was something like  this they were nearly identical which  explains why you know I had trouble  fitting the model so this is something  good to implement early you know again  test your assumptions what I learned  from this was ok I didn't quite  understand the date of the way that I  thought I did I need to go  and you know pull it again differently  another thing that I think is very  useful is to establish a baseline so I  would say like a good baseline is easy  to implement easy to understand and it's  simple but it's not a straw man right so  this should be when I think of a  baseline it's like something that if you  had to you could probably deploy within  a sprint and you would be confident that  it's working correctly and this is just  you know your base performance if any  more complicated model can't you know  beat your baseline then maybe you need  to go back and revisit the data  understanding revisit the business  understanding or or you know maybe you  just don't have the the resources you  need to be able to solve the problem at  that time where you need to frame the  problem differently so some examples a  simple heuristic so a concrete example  of this is I used to work in healthcare  and one of the models that we were  interested in there was if somebody had  this disease last year will he have it  this year and so you know a heuristic  for that is there's lots of diseases and  conditions that we know are chronic so  we could say well if this person had  this condition last year this chronic  condition we assume they have it this  year you know it's kind of on the  borderline of that like simple but kind  of a straw man because you know it's not  going to work for all the conditions but  it still kind of gets you in the  ballpark of what you should expect a  model to be able to be another example  is we predict ratings at Caden and so  one of the things is like that's just a  simple heuristic to form a baseline is  you know well let's just assume that  like the ratings on cable aren't really  changing much week over week so let's  use last week's we rating and use that  as our prediction and be honest it's a  it's a pretty good baseline to use  because they actually don't change much  week over week there's things like  seasonality where that that approach  will break down but again it gives you a  ballpark of where you want to be  an interpretable model is another great  example so you know something like a  linear model logistic regression linear  regression something like a tree based  model where you could inspect the  features again just to give you an idea  that you're on the right track so  another example I won't really go into  the details of this project but I worked  on a project and one feature kept  shooting up to the top and I couldn't  explain why it was unexpected and then  you know come to find out that that  feature was actually leaking information  about the target fail variable  information that we wouldn't have at  runtime so you know would have caused an  issue if it was something that we  deployed the production and was  something we were able to catch early so  that's sort of another side benefit you  can get from using an interpretable  model another good example is like in  the keynote right we saw just recently  you know the speaker took like these  deep learning models and compared it  against something like XG boost where  most of us are probably familiar with  you know gradient boosted trees and that  sort of thing and again it's a good  baseline to say you know there's a lot  of like costs they're deploying some of  these deep learning models well it  should definitely be able to do better  than something that's a little bit  simpler if it's going to be worth the  cost of putting into production and it  was so that's good it's you know  reaffirming that yes this deep learning  model is really worth the effort if you  have an existing model or methodology  some sort of business logic or heuristic  that's already in production obviously  this is your baseline and if you have an  acceptance criteria like this model if  the precision is less than 80 percent or  whatever it may be it's not going to be  useful you know that's like your  baseline and then of course you just  choose like whichever one might be the  best out of all these or but whichever  one you want to work with again it's  like a playbook not a not a rule book  here so another topic that's useful to  think about in terms of model evaluation  is cross validation which is usually  when we see it it's introduced as like  k-fold cross-validation we all kind of  know how that works right  like partition your data into say ten  ten sets ten partitions and trained on  nine tests on wall and trainer now in  testicle in one and that's good  one thing though that we found useful at  Kayden is the a form of cross-validation  that sort of replicates the deployment  pattern a little more closely and this  is something that looks a little bit  more like back testing if you've seen  that if you know you've worked in  finance or you know looked at like  trading algorithms that sort of thing  basically what they'll do there is you  know they'll go back and simulate how  would my model have done in the market  you know if I deployed it six months ago  and so we often do a similar thing here  where you know to keep it really simple  let's say our deployment pattern is  we're going to train on one month  predict on the next month then train on  that month predict on the next so your  cross-validation could be like train on  January predict on February train on  February predict on March you know it's  pretty easy to implement it avoids  leakage from future examples right into  your cross-validation the models only  ever to see what only ever able to see  what happened in the past during train  time which is good and it's more like  what's going to happen in reality when  you start training this model for  production another useful example is  sometimes it's worth training on a  cleaner data set so you know if you have  a data set that you know may be a little  bit noisy or something like that you  have an algorithm that say you're a  little bit unfamiliar with this is where  I found this to be useful you sort of  want to separate that noise in the data  set from the error that you see at train  time so to give a concrete example again  I mentioned we predicted ratings right  which could be formulated in terms of  like time series and deep learnings all  the rage right now right so you know the  idea I had as well maybe we could use  like an LSD N or a convolutional neural  network or something like that right to  solve this problem it seems like  something that we could try  and so our team walked into this one of  the things I looked into in the  beginning was well I didn't do this in  the beginning I wish I did so what I did  was we're really interested in  predicting some sort of rating that only  makes sense inside of our own internal  Cayden you know business structure which  is a little bit noisy but we have access  to national ratings which is much  cleaner and the two data sets that are  very analogous to one another a lot of  the same features are shared between the  two data sets so you know I could try  those approaches using CNN or an LST or  something like that on the national data  set something that I know is a little  bit cleaner that I know if I'm seeing  all sorts of weird errors it's probably  I've implemented the model wrong or I  need more regularization or something  like that and it's not just yeah this  data is noisy I didn't pull it correctly  I missed something or you know so yeah  that's it on that now we hit deployment  and beyond and again this is what really  matters right we really care about  performance in production and this is  there's a lot of things to say about  this phase but I guess I'll kind of talk  about just two things in this talk is  one you really want to ensure that your  model is useful and that it stays useful  and you want to be able to iterate with  confidence and so of course once you  deploy a model it's a production you  know you're tying the predictions to the  outcomes and you're gonna do some  evaluations but there are things and  that's like a no-brainer and we're  probably all doing that it's a good  first step  here's some other things that I think  are useful in addition to that so one is  a be testing I'm not really going to go  into a whole lot of details on a/b  testing I assume oops most people here  are used to it or familiar with the idea  but I this is a great pattern so why  would you want to do a/b testing so  let's assume this plot is like the  models error over time in production and  this red line represents a new release  right so it kind of looks like the  models error has dropped since the new  release  but has it really like we're data  scientists right don't we want to give  like a more like scientific answer as to  just saying like yeah we looked at the  plot it dropped because who knows like  maybe it's going to rise up again in the  future  you don't know so a/b testing is like a  great mechanism where you can deploy  iteratively you know send 5% of your  traffic to the new model 95% of traffic  to the existing model and start doing  comparisons and eventually just ramp up  the traffic that you're sending to the  new model as you gain confidence in it  you know while you're performing these  hypothesis tests until eventually the  new iteration is handling all of the  traffic and one other thing that I think  is the last thing I'm going to mention  is monitoring which you know could cover  a wide range of things but some of the  things that I think you would really  want to think about monitoring it's like  monitoring the the input data looking  for things like covariant shift and  stuff like that where the distribution  of the data at runtime is different from  the distribution of the data at train  time and you know that obviously is like  a topic all in itself two useful  guidelines that came from a paper on  tensorflow extended which is sort of  like you know put out by the tensorflow  developers it's sort of like production  pipeline kind of infrastructure thing  for tensorflow models as they said you  know keep it simple and keep it  actionable and I think this is useful  right so in an example they give is like  if you're monitoring for deviations in  runtime data from train time data you  know favor looking at ranges over  something like coal back Leibler  divergence right like if you look at you  know if you get a report and says the KL  divergence is greater than this or  exceeded some threshold like how it's  hard to understand that and it's also  hard to understand what the action you  should take us but if you get an alert  that says for this numeric feature we  saw values that are  you know five times greater than the  largest value we saw during training  that's a little bit more actionable  that's something that you can look into  a little bit more often you can monitor  how many times those things happen or  look you know if you're receiving input  from categorical variables and you're  seeing new values that you never saw  before there may be legitimate reasons  for that perhaps you have new vendors  new products whatever but maybe it's an  indication that you need to retrain the  model so that it can sync with the data  that it's currently seeing at run time  and another good thing too that I think  as a guideline to try and reimplemented  each X that you looked at during  development during this phase so I  actually had a slide on sanity checks  and I removed it but I would just say  sign each X I think we were all doing  them already  during training is you know we're  looking at future importances and/or  coefficients of the models things like  that making sure they make sense we're  looking at the ranges that of like say  we're doing regression we're looking at  the ranges of what the models predicting  making sure that we're not predicting  things that are you know way large  bigger than what we would really expect  to see and those sanity checks could be  reintroduced sort of in the post  deployment phase where you could monitor  for those things as well so if you've  already integrated them into your  workflow during training they're great  things to carry over here maybe you can  hand them off to QA or something like  that and those are things they can check  for and so that's it right now  so in conclusion hopefully there are  some takeaways one of the things that I  appreciate about these conferences is I  think you know there's a lot of  opportunity to learn from other people  who are doing this and so hopefully  you've learned something and I would  love for you to share your suggestions  this is definitely an area that I could  grow in I think the team I work with we  can all grow in I think this industry  can grow in so you know afterwards I'd  love to hear what sort of practices you  guys have in development  and lastly there's a link here in the  slides which I think the conference will  link to the conference will link to the  slides and slides link to a blog post  where I took basically the contents of  this talk and I put it into a post and  it's on my blog and there's a little bit  more information there so if you want  more that's where it is and I guess now  we can open up for questions  [Applause]
 

